From: Leon Zandman <leon@wirwar.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 23:04:46 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] 09993: Parent dialogs to the tool's last active window when
 no window is active

Parent dialogs to the tool's last active window when no
 window is active

When a dialog is shown without a parent and Java reports no active window,
getParentWindow() fell back to the tool's root frame. On macOS this happens
after Delete Block in a detached Memory Map window: the progress dialog is
shown right after the confirmation dialog closes, before the OS has made the
Memory Map window active again. The progress dialog got the CodeBrowser frame
as its owner, and that frame came to the front when the dialog closed.

getParentWindow() now tries the tool's last active window before the root
frame. The existing walk up the component hierarchy moved into
getNonTransientWindowFor(), so a closed, modal or transient last window
resolves to its owner, and windows that cannot be parents are still skipped.
Use case 'F' in the method's comment describes the new rule.

DialogParentFallbackTest covers the fallback with a fake focus manager.

Fixes #9963

Fall back to the last active window in getJavaActiveWindow()

Move the fallback from getParentWindow() into getJavaActiveWindow(), as
suggested in review, so that it also applies to centering the dialog and
to parenting non-modal dialogs.

The fallback is now also used when Java still reports a window that has
just closed as the active window. On macOS this happens after Delete Block
in a detached Memory Map: sometimes Java reports no active window, and
sometimes the closed confirmation dialog.

When the last active window is itself a closed dialog, walk up to the
nearest owner that is still showing. Otherwise the new dialog gets the
right parent but is centered on the screen.

DialogParentFallbackTest has new cases for a closed dialog as the active
window and for centering on the owner.
